  "image_digest": "sha256:0be6e16e2eb288dfd5fa8b0b41015f61731a139fb961d3366ccedf834289d781",
  "key_findings": [
    "KVCOMM also has a body-length crossover: target-only is slower than dense at body512/768, but reaches about 1.37x at body1024 and 1.76x at body2048 under rho~2.",
    "Body1024 target-only speedup remains about 1.36-1.38x from peak rho~1.03 through 3.11 with real eviction.",
    "Body2048 setup is about 2.16s but target saves about 422ms, giving an amortized break-even near 6 target reuses; body1024 breaks even near 14 reuses.",
    "Larger headers increase both setup and target absolute latency, but relative target-only speedup improves from about 1.30x at header0 to 1.46x at header256.",
    "Each 512-token placeholder has a target canonical base and two independently encoded anchor bases plus two context deltas; physical setup footprint is roughly 5x body tokens before the target reconstruction buffer."
  ],
  "known_limitations": [
    "The unified long-body runner omits neighboring-prefix delta groups; the earlier small KVCOMM canary remains the evidence for that mechanism.",
    "Long bodies are represented as multiple independent placeholder pools, not one contiguous canonical source object.",
    "Setup is excluded from target-only TTFT and is reported with explicit break-even request counts.",
    "No accuracy or output-equivalence metric was collected.",
    "Results are single-model SM75 measurements and require RTX PRO 6000/H100 replication."
  ],
